# Writing pages

A page is just a `.work` file of prose. The first heading is its title; everything under it renders
in order. You write Markdown — headings, lists, **bold**, `inline code`, fenced code blocks, and
> blockquotes for callouts.

## Sections and order

The sidebar mirrors `index.work` exactly. Sections group pages; pages appear in the order you list
them. To reorder, move the `page` lines — nothing else.

## Links and refs

- A Markdown link to a real page path (`[text](/section/page)`) navigates inside the site.
- An external link (`https://…`) opens normally.

## Live blocks (optional)

Prose is the floor, not the ceiling. Because every page is a real workbook, you can drop a `client`
island into one to embed something interactive — a diagram, a demo, a calculator — right between two
paragraphs. Start with prose; reach for a block only when a page needs to *do* something.

## Keep it yours

Delete the starter pages once you've read them. The only file you can't delete is `index.work` — it's
the table of contents. Everything else is yours to write.
